WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.472
[ Watch on KissFlix.co ]

2
00:04.742 --> 00:07.178
Two months until the Hakone Ekiden.

3
00:07.245 --> 00:09.347
The Student United Team, made up of runners from teams

4
00:09.414 --> 00:12.117
that failed to qualify, begins training.

5
00:12.183 --> 00:14.853
My goal is a top-three finish at the Hakone Ekiden.

6
00:14.919 --> 00:16.121
A top-three finish?

7
00:16.187 --> 00:19.724
Some resist Coach Masato Kai's unconventional methods,

8
00:19.791 --> 00:21.092
as he has no coaching experience.

9
00:21.159 --> 00:23.027
You'd be crazy to take this seriously!

10
00:23.094 --> 00:25.096
Don't mess with me!

11
00:25.163 --> 00:26.865
We've lost once already!

12
00:26.931 --> 00:28.867
That's exactly why we can run like no one else!

13
00:28.933 --> 00:31.035
Meanwhile, at Dainichi TV…

14
00:31.102 --> 00:34.005
This is what we call the Hakone Ekiden Broadcast Handbook.

15
00:34.072 --> 00:37.041
It's essentially a manual for broadcasting the Hakone Ekiden.

16
00:37.108 --> 00:41.179
Chief producer Ryo Tokushige clashed with upper management.

17
00:41.246 --> 00:43.414
Hakone Ekiden belongs to the runners.

18
00:43.481 --> 00:46.251
Perfectionist broadcast director

19
00:46.818 --> 00:49.020
Natsuki Miyamoto also clashed with her crew.

20
00:49.087 --> 00:50.722
Is the broadcast director some kind of god?

21
00:50.788 --> 00:53.691
Maybe I'm not cut out for this job.

22
00:53.758 --> 00:55.293
Maybe don't toss it all,

23
00:56.060 --> 00:57.095
but try tossing a little?

24
00:57.162 --> 00:59.197
-I'll go! -Please go.

25
00:59.264 --> 01:00.565
Meanwhile…

26
01:00.632 --> 01:03.034
Maeda will probably be hospitalized.

27
01:03.768 --> 01:08.206
Another ordeal awaited those already facing adversity.

28
01:08.273 --> 01:09.741
This is bad…

29
01:17.148 --> 01:20.985
CHIEF PRODUCER RYO TOKUSHIGE

30
01:21.052 --> 01:22.353
What about the evening news?

31
01:22.420 --> 01:24.789
For now, the weekday sub-anchors

32
01:24.856 --> 01:26.457
can fill in.

33
01:27.025 --> 01:28.393
But going forward…

34
01:28.459 --> 01:29.294
ANNOUNCING DIRECTOR PUBLIC RELATIONS DIRECTOR

35
01:29.360 --> 01:30.195
DEPUTY NEWS DIRECTOR

36
01:30.261 --> 01:31.196
Come in.

37
01:31.262 --> 01:32.931
Excuse me.

38
01:34.332 --> 01:35.433
Sorry I'm late.

39
01:35.500 --> 01:37.502
PROGRAMMING BUREAU CHIEF TAKESHI KUROISHI

40
01:39.504 --> 01:42.106
Is Maeda going to be okay?

41
01:42.674 --> 01:46.244
Esophageal cancer. Stage three.

42
01:47.278 --> 01:48.880
Stage three…

43
01:49.814 --> 01:52.417
The Hakone Ekiden is just two months away, isn't it?

44
01:52.483 --> 01:54.719
Who's going to fill in?

45
01:55.687 --> 01:57.255
Someone to replace Maeda…

46
01:58.122 --> 02:01.092
No, I really can't think of anyone.

47
02:01.159 --> 02:03.228
More than 20 announcers

48
02:03.294 --> 02:05.797
work on the Hakone Ekiden broadcast.

49
02:05.863 --> 02:09.267
The broadcast announcer leads

50
02:09.334 --> 02:11.769
the play-by-play announcers in the field.

51
02:11.836 --> 02:13.705
The command center, so to speak.

52
02:13.771 --> 02:17.408
It's no exaggeration to say their skill decides

53
02:17.475 --> 02:18.676
whether the broadcast succeeds.

54
02:18.743 --> 02:20.678
SHIRAKAWA HOSPITAL

55
02:21.246 --> 02:22.180
Mr. Tokushige,

56
02:22.914 --> 02:24.215
I'm so sorry.

57
02:25.250 --> 02:26.284
Don't worry about it.

58
02:26.985 --> 02:29.153
Besides that, how are you feeling?

59
02:29.754 --> 02:33.524
I just feel a little off.

60
02:34.559 --> 02:38.029
But now I'm starting radiation and chemo.

61
02:39.497 --> 02:40.632
I see.

62
02:41.733 --> 02:45.803
I was really looking forward to doing Hakone with this team.

63
02:45.870 --> 02:46.904
HISASHI MAEDA

64
02:49.707 --> 02:50.875
I'm so frustrated…

65
02:53.645 --> 02:54.512
Me too.

66
02:55.913 --> 02:59.517
Right now, your job is to focus on getting better.

67
03:00.551 --> 03:03.187
When you're well, we'll do it again.

68
03:04.656 --> 03:05.556
Okay.

69
03:06.891 --> 03:08.660
Um, Mr. Tokushige.

70
03:09.193 --> 03:12.430
What's going to happen about my replacement?

71
03:14.732 --> 03:15.833
I'll think about it.

72
03:17.001 --> 03:22.173
It's not my place to say, but without enough time to prepare,

73
03:22.240 --> 03:26.177
Hakone will be tough for anyone without real experience.

74
03:28.146 --> 03:29.147
You're right.

75
03:31.015 --> 03:36.220
Well… It's not like I have no one in mind.

76
03:37.355 --> 03:38.389
Mr. Karashima?

77
03:43.828 --> 03:47.665
I was thinking the same thing. I hope he will do it.

78
03:50.101 --> 03:52.970
Yeah, I know…

79
03:53.037 --> 03:55.740
Mr. Karashima? You mean that Mr. Karashima?!

80
03:55.807 --> 03:58.076
Come on, Mr. Tokushige. Are you serious?

81
03:58.142 --> 03:59.844
You know what he's like.

82
03:59.911 --> 04:01.646
This is bad.

83
04:02.380 --> 04:03.514
Um, sorry.

84
04:03.581 --> 04:06.584
Is Mr. Karashima really that bad?

85
04:08.786 --> 04:10.388
He's really that bad.

86
04:10.455 --> 04:13.758
Basically, he's a pain.

87
04:13.825 --> 04:16.861
He expects the crew to know more than the experts.

88
04:16.928 --> 04:19.430
Plenty of directors have run from him.

89
04:19.497 --> 04:21.332
He chewed me out once too.

90
04:21.399 --> 04:23.134
"There’s no such phrase as 'fixed concept'."

91
04:23.201 --> 04:25.069
"It's either 'fixed idea' or 'preconception'."

92
04:25.136 --> 04:26.204
Could you say that again?

93
04:28.506 --> 04:30.842
I said, "Sorry, I should've studied more."

94
04:30.908 --> 04:32.643
"Not sorry. I'm sorry."

95
04:32.710 --> 04:35.947
"Can't you even apologize properly?" And that set him off again.

96
04:36.013 --> 04:37.448
I'm traumatized.

97
04:37.515 --> 04:41.119
He handled my new-hire training too.

98
04:41.185 --> 04:44.522
He drilled me for two hours on how I pronounce my own name.

99
04:44.589 --> 04:45.456
Two hours?!

100
04:45.523 --> 04:48.726
He's got all three Hs. Harsh, hard-nosed and hard to please.

101
04:48.793 --> 04:51.028
And he never takes a job he doesn't like.

102
04:51.095 --> 04:52.764
How does that even work?

103
04:52.830 --> 04:55.166
Office workers are supposed to do the jobs they're given.

104
04:55.233 --> 04:57.268
50 DAYS UNTIL HAKONE EKIDEN

105
04:57.335 --> 04:58.770
I'm for it.

106
05:00.171 --> 05:02.774
Mr. Karashima is a pro at golf broadcasts.

107
05:02.840 --> 05:06.077
He's suited to Hakone, where the competition happens all at once.

108
05:10.782 --> 05:13.451
Well, only those of us inside

109
05:13.518 --> 05:15.586
know how nasty he is.

110
05:16.120 --> 05:18.456
On air, he sounds smooth,

111
05:18.523 --> 05:21.426
and somehow he never comes off as snide.

112
05:21.492 --> 05:23.161
Is that praise or an insult?

113
05:24.595 --> 05:28.132
He's hard to work with, sure. But if we put up with that,

114
05:28.199 --> 05:31.836
I think… it'll be a great broadcast.

115
05:32.403 --> 05:34.939
-You think? -But can you put up with him,

116
05:36.040 --> 05:37.275
Mr. Tokushige?

117
05:39.210 --> 05:40.111
Anyway,

118
05:40.945 --> 05:42.046
no time like the present.

119
05:42.547 --> 05:44.449
TATEYAMA LADIES OPEN

120
05:58.262 --> 05:59.263
Mr. Karashima,

121
06:00.064 --> 06:01.132
it's been a while.

122
06:02.533 --> 06:06.003
On the final day, they'll put the pin by this pond.

123
06:07.371 --> 06:09.941
This hole decides the tournament.

124
06:12.276 --> 06:13.144
Okay…

125
06:15.379 --> 06:16.714
What do you want?

126
06:19.517 --> 06:23.387
Actually, I have a favor to ask.

127
06:24.755 --> 06:27.992
Would you cover the Hakone Ekiden?

128
06:29.494 --> 06:31.796
Why not let one of the young ones do it?

129
06:34.432 --> 06:35.900
If someone young does it,

130
06:36.801 --> 06:39.237
I want to give them time to prepare.

131
06:40.805 --> 06:41.939
You know me.

132
06:42.607 --> 06:46.878
I can't stand sappy sports broadcasts.

133
06:50.414 --> 06:52.416
15 YEARS AGO

134
06:52.483 --> 06:56.187
Mr. Karashima, could you tell this story about the runner's family next?

135
06:56.888 --> 06:58.523
FAMILY STORY

136
06:59.090 --> 07:00.992
Sorry. Not now.

137
07:02.360 --> 07:05.162
-Hey, Tokushige! -Yes!

138
07:05.229 --> 07:06.998
The family story's set, right?

139
07:07.064 --> 07:08.699
Next is Car Three.

140
07:08.766 --> 07:11.068
Uh, I'm sorry, but…

141
07:11.135 --> 07:14.205
Mr. Karashima doesn't seem willing to…

142
07:14.272 --> 07:16.807
What? Hey!

143
07:17.441 --> 07:20.211
Is he griping again?

144
07:20.278 --> 07:22.146
If not now, when?

145
07:22.213 --> 07:23.047
Shut up, Kitamura!

146
07:23.114 --> 07:24.749
You're annoying, Kitamura!

147
07:24.815 --> 07:26.417
What are you even watching?

148
07:26.484 --> 07:28.319
Look at the runner's head!

149
07:28.386 --> 07:31.656
It's swaying side to side. That means he's tired.

150
07:31.722 --> 07:33.524
This is where the race is decided!

151
07:33.591 --> 07:34.825
And you're forcing a link

152
07:34.892 --> 07:39.764
between his family's tragedy and his running?

153
07:39.830 --> 07:42.833
"His father is cheering him on from heaven"?

154
07:42.900 --> 07:45.469
What is that? That's ridiculous!

155
07:46.037 --> 07:48.606
It doesn't matter how perfect

156
07:49.307 --> 07:50.708
a script you prepare.

157
07:51.542 --> 07:54.412
We cover the athletes competing

158
07:55.179 --> 07:57.048
at this moment.

159
07:58.549 --> 08:01.852
That's supposed to be our job.

160
08:02.820 --> 08:03.688
Isn't it?

161
08:06.991 --> 08:10.328
Well, no wonder a pain-in-the-neck old man

162
08:11.429 --> 08:12.897
like me gets fewer jobs.

163
08:16.767 --> 08:17.668
See you.

164
08:24.408 --> 08:28.045
10,000-METER TIME TRIAL MEET

165
08:28.613 --> 08:30.114
A MEET WHERE UNIVERSITY ATHLETES COMPETE

166
08:30.181 --> 08:32.350
FOR RECORDS AND PERSONAL BESTS

167
08:33.184 --> 08:35.419
-Everyone, gather round. -Yes.

168
08:38.055 --> 08:41.425
I'll announce your legs this weekend.

169
08:42.026 --> 08:44.261
-Leg assignments… -This is it…

170
08:44.328 --> 08:45.896
Today's 10,000-meter race

171
08:45.963 --> 08:48.633
will be a big factor in deciding them.

172
08:48.699 --> 08:51.469
Don't let up. Give it everything.

173
08:51.969 --> 08:52.837
That's all.

174
08:52.903 --> 08:53.938
-Yes. -Yes.

175
08:57.842 --> 09:00.011
TOZAI UNIVERSITY

176
09:00.077 --> 09:01.112
SHOSUKE HIRAKAWA

177
09:01.178 --> 09:03.447
-Coach Hirakawa of Tozai University! -Yeah!

178
09:03.514 --> 09:04.949
Mr. Hirakawa, do you have a moment?

179
09:05.016 --> 09:06.584
He's got quite a presence!

180
09:06.651 --> 09:08.352
Hey, let's go get his autograph later.

181
09:08.419 --> 09:10.554
Yeah, let's go.

182
09:10.621 --> 09:12.623
Two titles in a row for sure, right?

183
09:12.690 --> 09:16.093
Still, it depends on the other teams.

184
09:16.160 --> 09:18.929
-We can't get careless. -I see.

185
09:23.067 --> 09:24.669
Long time no see, Mr. Kai.

186
09:25.670 --> 09:27.505
You all know him.

187
09:27.571 --> 09:30.141
Coach Kai of the Student United Team.

188
09:30.708 --> 09:31.542
I'm Kai.

189
09:32.543 --> 09:35.446
You two were rivals in college, weren't you?

190
09:35.513 --> 09:36.480
No.

191
09:36.547 --> 09:40.184
We ran the same leg three times. Izumo, All-Japan and Hakone.

192
09:40.251 --> 09:41.919
He beat me all three times.

193
09:41.986 --> 09:44.422
Calling us rivals would be presumptuous.

194
09:44.488 --> 09:46.524
But as a head coach,

195
09:46.590 --> 09:50.394
my record's a little better.

196
09:51.162 --> 09:54.331
Of course. Tozai's the reigning champ.

197
09:54.398 --> 09:57.268
This time, you'll face off

198
09:57.334 --> 09:58.836
as head coaches.

199
09:58.903 --> 10:00.504
How are you feeling about it?

200
10:02.506 --> 10:03.808
Speaking of feelings,

201
10:03.874 --> 10:08.679
the Student United Team seems fired up.

202
10:09.246 --> 10:11.415
What was your goal again?

203
10:12.216 --> 10:13.617
Top three in the main race.

204
10:15.720 --> 10:18.889
Be careful, Mr. Kai.

205
10:19.557 --> 10:20.891
You know the old saying.

206
10:21.692 --> 10:24.161
"A great player

207
10:24.228 --> 10:26.197
doesn't always make a great coach."

208
10:26.263 --> 10:28.699
-Right? -Yes.

209
10:29.400 --> 10:32.470
Well, go easy on us.

210
10:33.604 --> 10:34.972
Let's make it an exciting race.

211
10:37.341 --> 10:39.643
See you at Hakone.

212
10:43.114 --> 10:46.851
Next, men's 10,000-meter Heat 6.

213
10:50.454 --> 10:52.623
TOZAI UNIVERSITY SANNO UNIVERSITY

214
10:52.690 --> 10:55.526
MEISEI GAKUIN UNIVERSITY

215
11:26.190 --> 11:27.491
-Looking good! -You got this!

216
11:27.558 --> 11:29.894
Keep that pace!

217
11:29.960 --> 11:31.262
You got this!

218
11:31.328 --> 11:33.597
Follow him!

219
11:36.066 --> 11:37.968
Come on!

220
11:38.035 --> 11:40.538
Kota and the others are flying…

221
11:40.604 --> 11:41.505
‎MEISEI GAKUIN UNIVERSITY TEAM MANAGER ‎KEITO YANO

222
11:54.185 --> 11:55.886
MEISEI GAKUIN HAKONE EKIDEN QUALIFIER

223
11:58.122 --> 12:02.092
11TH PLACE-MEISEI GAKUIN UNIVERSITY

224
12:02.159 --> 12:03.594
I'm sorry, everyone.

225
12:03.661 --> 12:05.529
It's my fault.

226
12:09.400 --> 12:10.968
Huh?

227
12:11.035 --> 12:13.337
Hayato's pace suddenly dropped…

228
13:07.791 --> 13:09.593
-Are everyone's times in? -Yes.

229
13:10.928 --> 13:13.397
-For me… -Man,

230
13:13.464 --> 13:15.332
I could've cut five more seconds.

231
13:15.399 --> 13:17.601
I fell behind at the end too.

232
13:18.335 --> 13:19.203
What's this?

233
13:19.937 --> 13:20.771
Huh?

234
13:20.838 --> 13:23.941
"We'll do things our way."

235
13:24.008 --> 13:26.677
These three have great times! Especially Kota!

236
13:26.744 --> 13:29.079
-You're right. -Isn't that weird?

237
13:29.146 --> 13:31.348
Then why is he like that at camp?

238
13:32.883 --> 13:35.719
Maybe he didn't want to get injured.

239
13:35.786 --> 13:38.155
The Student United Team has Daichi Murai

240
13:38.222 --> 13:39.623
-from Toho Economics too. -Yeah.

241
13:39.690 --> 13:41.125
They look pretty strong this year.

242
13:41.825 --> 13:44.461
Yeah, everyone's surprisingly fired up. Even the coach and captain.

243
13:44.528 --> 13:46.897
The captain's Mr. Aoba from Meisei Gakuin, right?

244
13:46.964 --> 13:47.965
Oh, yeah.

245
13:48.032 --> 13:50.134
Isn't he the guy who blew the qualifying race?

246
13:50.200 --> 13:51.068
Why is he captain?

247
13:51.702 --> 13:54.104
Well, the coach is from Meisei too.

248
13:54.805 --> 13:55.973
-Right. -Yeah.

249
13:56.040 --> 13:58.542
-That makes things easier. -Yeah.

250
14:13.023 --> 14:15.993
Wow, this is a nice place.

251
14:16.060 --> 14:18.495
-It's got character. -Right.

252
14:19.063 --> 14:21.765
-I guess. -It's just old.

253
14:26.036 --> 14:27.972
Um, Mr. Karashima…

254
14:28.706 --> 14:31.542
Honestly, this broadcast is in crisis.

255
14:31.608 --> 14:32.476
YOSHIO KITAMURA

256
14:32.543 --> 14:35.579
You're the only one who can save it.

257
14:36.280 --> 14:38.716
We're sure of that.

258
14:41.085 --> 14:43.354
Right, Bureau Chief Kitamura?

259
14:43.921 --> 14:46.490
Well, they're asking this earnestly.

260
14:46.557 --> 14:48.625
-Please do it. -No.

261
14:48.692 --> 14:50.094
Hakone and I don't get along.

262
14:50.894 --> 14:53.130
-No… -You once said

263
14:53.197 --> 14:55.532
my directing was over the top.

264
14:55.599 --> 14:58.369
But it got ratings back then, didn't it?

265
14:58.435 --> 15:00.838
Viewers are honest.

266
15:01.538 --> 15:04.341
It's not about ratings.

267
15:04.408 --> 15:06.844
Your directing is a gimmick

268
15:06.910 --> 15:09.213
-that strays from the heart of sports. -A gimmick?

269
15:09.780 --> 15:11.949
Watch your mouth.

270
15:12.016 --> 15:12.916
Then it's a travesty.

271
15:12.983 --> 15:16.687
Um, that was 15 years ago, right?

272
15:18.088 --> 15:21.458
Let's let bygones be bygones…

273
15:21.525 --> 15:25.562
Listen. I've always tried to see sports

274
15:25.629 --> 15:27.297
as sports, with no bias.

275
15:28.198 --> 15:30.200
Forcing the runners into a script

276
15:30.267 --> 15:34.204
we prepared ahead of time?

277
15:34.271 --> 15:37.408
I won't do a tame sports broadcast like that.

278
15:37.474 --> 15:38.809
You gotta be kidding!

279
15:38.876 --> 15:41.478
I'm impressed.

280
15:41.545 --> 15:44.214
You're the most stubborn man in Japan.

281
15:44.281 --> 15:46.850
I don't want to hear that from you.

282
15:52.289 --> 15:55.492
-Good work. -Good work.

283
15:55.559 --> 15:57.327
-Good work. -Good work.

284
15:58.729 --> 16:01.732
-Good work. -Nomura! It's been ages!

285
16:01.799 --> 16:04.034
I saw the favorite announcer rankings.

286
16:04.101 --> 16:05.569
Number one. Congrats!

287
16:05.636 --> 16:08.505
I've just been lucky with my shows.

288
16:08.572 --> 16:11.742
Famous but still humble. I could learn from you.

289
16:13.077 --> 16:14.445
So what's up? You need me?

290
16:15.913 --> 16:16.914
Could you come with me?

291
16:20.651 --> 16:24.221
Marumaru Hour is doing great.

292
16:24.288 --> 16:25.622
It's all thanks to you, Maruta.

293
16:26.457 --> 16:31.261
Thank you. I'm really grateful to be on such a good show.

294
16:33.130 --> 16:37.301
By the way, Maruta. I want to ask you something.

295
16:37.367 --> 16:38.302
What is it?

296
16:39.536 --> 16:42.773
Will you be the broadcast announcer for the Hakone Ekiden?

297
16:44.007 --> 16:46.043
What? Me?

298
16:48.112 --> 16:50.681
Maeda's in the hospital, so we're looking for a replacement.

299
16:50.747 --> 16:53.283
You started out in sports, right? You're perfect.

300
16:55.052 --> 16:56.753
Honestly,

301
16:57.287 --> 17:00.357
I find the current Hakone broadcast lacking.

302
17:00.424 --> 17:02.426
They pour in all those people and money,

303
17:02.493 --> 17:04.695
and it's as dull as it gets.

304
17:06.730 --> 17:07.965
Listen, Maruta.

305
17:09.066 --> 17:11.235
What we need is impact.

306
17:12.503 --> 17:15.539
An unconventional broadcast to bring new life to Hakone.

307
17:16.473 --> 17:17.374
What do you think?

308
17:18.642 --> 17:20.277
Doesn't that sound exciting?

309
17:35.592 --> 17:38.629
Why did Bureau Chief Kitamura even come tonight?

310
17:39.396 --> 17:42.666
How can two people get along that badly?

311
17:45.836 --> 17:47.638
Mr. Tokushige, what should we do?

312
17:49.406 --> 17:50.908
What do you think, Miyamoto?

313
17:52.442 --> 17:56.847
I still think Mr. Karashima is the only one who can be broadcast announcer.

314
17:57.714 --> 17:59.883
Sure, he's stubborn and twisted.

315
17:59.950 --> 18:02.186
He's picky and has a nasty mouth,

316
18:02.252 --> 18:04.555
-and his ideas are old-fashioned… -Hey, now…

317
18:05.989 --> 18:07.424
But as a sports announcer,

318
18:08.959 --> 18:10.360
Mr. Karashima has held on

319
18:10.427 --> 18:13.130
to his principles for years.

320
18:14.331 --> 18:16.867
Respect for the runners.

321
18:17.834 --> 18:19.136
I think that's what

322
18:19.203 --> 18:21.371
we should value most

323
18:21.438 --> 18:24.575
when we broadcast the Hakone Ekiden.

324
18:28.478 --> 18:29.980
I think so too.

325
18:33.383 --> 18:35.586
PROGRAMMING BUREAU CHIEF KUROISHI

326
18:37.154 --> 18:37.988
Bureau Chief Kuroishi.

327
18:42.392 --> 18:43.627
Hello.

328
18:43.694 --> 18:46.363
What's happening with the Hakone broadcast announcer?

329
18:47.497 --> 18:51.134
If it's not settled yet, there's someone I'd like to recommend.

330
18:52.369 --> 18:53.437
Who is it?

331
18:54.338 --> 18:55.839
How about Maruta?

332
18:56.807 --> 18:58.075
Maruta?

333
18:58.875 --> 19:01.678
With a variety-show host like him,

334
19:02.246 --> 19:04.615
the broadcast will be five times more exciting.

335
19:05.482 --> 19:07.284
Will it?

336
19:08.118 --> 19:09.219
He's popular, sure, but--

337
19:09.286 --> 19:11.121
How do we get viewers to love

338
19:11.922 --> 19:15.826
the Hakone Ekiden even more?

339
19:15.892 --> 19:17.561
Think hard with that thick skull of yours.

340
19:18.929 --> 19:20.764
Listen, Tokushige.

341
19:20.831 --> 19:25.569
This isn't change. It's evolution.

342
19:26.336 --> 19:28.772
Maruta's eager to do it too.

343
19:30.173 --> 19:32.276
-Mr. Kuroishi… -Got it?

344
19:36.780 --> 19:38.482
Was that about Maruta?

345
19:47.858 --> 19:50.294
Okay. Good work.

346
19:53.363 --> 19:54.331
Thank you!

347
19:58.235 --> 20:00.937
Leg assignments are tomorrow, huh?

348
20:01.004 --> 20:03.040
I won't sleep a wink tonight.

349
20:03.106 --> 20:03.940
‎SANNO UNIVERSITY SECOND-YEAR STUDENT ‎DAN KURASHINA

350
20:05.242 --> 20:06.743
Haru, which leg did you ask for?

351
20:06.810 --> 20:07.644
HARUKA MOMOYAMA

352
20:07.711 --> 20:08.912
The seventh leg.

353
20:08.979 --> 20:11.014
Oh, same here.

354
20:11.782 --> 20:13.717
I guess that makes us rivals.

355
20:15.952 --> 20:17.154
Go…

356
20:18.055 --> 20:19.756
When some get to run,

357
20:20.390 --> 20:22.192
there are always others who don't.

358
20:23.727 --> 20:25.896
That's Hakone, right?

359
20:31.001 --> 20:33.103
Sorry, go on ahead.

360
20:33.704 --> 20:35.605
I'm going to do one more stride.

361
20:36.206 --> 20:37.307
Okay.

362
20:38.108 --> 20:39.776
-Keito, can you take this? -Of course.

363
20:39.843 --> 20:42.145
Thanks. See you.

364
20:57.461 --> 20:59.396
Um, Coach.

365
21:01.298 --> 21:03.867
Can I ask you about something?

366
21:05.902 --> 21:07.671
It's about Hayato.

367
21:08.305 --> 21:11.508
Lately, he keeps fading

368
21:11.575 --> 21:13.110
in the second half of races.

369
21:14.745 --> 21:17.314
Is there anything I can do?

370
21:20.784 --> 21:23.754
Let's try it in the evening.

371
21:24.388 --> 21:25.355
In the evening?

372
21:29.159 --> 21:31.528
Hayato! Wait!

373
21:33.230 --> 21:35.098
Let's do a few 400s.

374
21:35.165 --> 21:37.000
-What, now? -Yes, now!

375
21:37.067 --> 21:39.369
Oh, and take off your watch.

376
21:40.203 --> 21:41.538
Why all of a sudden?

377
21:42.839 --> 21:45.509
It feels just right!

378
21:48.311 --> 21:49.146
What does?

379
21:50.914 --> 21:51.882
First one.

380
21:52.549 --> 21:54.518
Ready, go!

381
22:14.604 --> 22:15.906
Now, the second one.

382
22:17.507 --> 22:19.242
Ready, go!

383
22:20.544 --> 22:22.145
Now, the third one.

384
22:23.447 --> 22:25.315
Ready, go!

385
22:58.682 --> 23:00.884
Hayato, that's amazing!

386
23:02.118 --> 23:03.320
Great time!

387
23:05.088 --> 23:06.323
You're right…

388
23:07.224 --> 23:09.159
It's been a while since you ran in the 65s.

389
23:10.026 --> 23:11.561
Come to think of it,

390
23:11.628 --> 23:13.964
running felt easier than usual.

391
23:14.030 --> 23:15.732
My body felt lighter.

392
23:17.067 --> 23:19.636
Let's time it again tomorrow morning.

393
23:20.904 --> 23:22.606
-Let's do that. -Yes.

394
23:29.579 --> 23:32.716
Did Bureau Chief Kuroishi tell you

395
23:33.316 --> 23:35.719
about the Hakone broadcast announcer?

396
23:35.785 --> 23:36.686
Yes.

397
23:38.321 --> 23:41.358
I wanted to know how you feel about it.

398
23:44.728 --> 23:48.031
Honestly, I was confused

399
23:48.932 --> 23:50.133
when he first asked me.

400
23:51.067 --> 23:53.637
I wondered if I could fill in for Mr. Maeda.

401
23:55.539 --> 23:58.141
But Mr. Kuroishi said he wants to see

402
23:58.208 --> 24:01.211
Hakone done my way.

403
24:03.680 --> 24:05.382
I plan to give Hakone

404
24:06.149 --> 24:09.085
everything I've got.

405
24:11.087 --> 24:13.757
Mr. Tokushige, I look forward to working with you.

406
24:24.000 --> 24:24.935
Maruta.

407
24:25.635 --> 24:26.636
Yes?

408
24:27.203 --> 24:29.673
I know how good

409
24:30.340 --> 24:31.341
an announcer you are.

410
24:32.442 --> 24:33.476
Your skill

411
24:34.044 --> 24:36.479
and your flair for drawing in viewers.

412
24:38.949 --> 24:39.783
But…

413
24:40.283 --> 24:42.953
But that's exactly why I'm worried.

414
24:45.755 --> 24:47.357
As you know,

415
24:48.058 --> 24:51.027
the Hakone Ekiden runners are the stars.

416
24:52.495 --> 24:55.165
That's the principle the broadcast

417
24:55.231 --> 24:57.867
has been built on all these years.

418
25:00.070 --> 25:03.607
If you're the broadcast announcer,

419
25:03.673 --> 25:08.912
the Hakone Ekiden will surely become Maruta's Hakone Ekiden.

420
25:16.586 --> 25:19.756
Is that a bad thing?

421
25:21.391 --> 25:25.729
TV must never change Hakone.

422
25:30.200 --> 25:31.301
Actually,

423
25:32.268 --> 25:37.807
I'm planning to ask Mr. Karashima to be broadcast announcer.

424
25:45.382 --> 25:46.583
Okay, last one.

425
25:47.117 --> 25:48.251
I'll record the time.

426
25:49.552 --> 25:51.821
Ready, go.

427
26:13.877 --> 26:14.711
Seriously?!

428
26:16.880 --> 26:17.714
How was it?

429
26:17.781 --> 26:19.516
That's amazing! You're faster!

430
26:29.092 --> 26:30.160
Keito,

431
26:31.761 --> 26:33.029
are you stopping

432
26:33.930 --> 26:35.899
the stopwatch early?

433
26:37.867 --> 26:39.903
-I didn't stop it early today! -Today?

434
26:42.806 --> 26:43.673
Oh, um…

435
26:46.309 --> 26:47.610
What do you mean?

436
26:51.548 --> 26:55.919
Actually, I talked to Coach Kai.

437
26:56.953 --> 26:59.923
You've been in a slump lately…

438
27:00.490 --> 27:03.793
Let's try it in the evening.

439
27:04.527 --> 27:05.428
In the evening?

440
27:06.262 --> 27:08.631
You run as it gets darker.

441
27:08.698 --> 27:12.102
What does that do?

442
27:13.303 --> 27:17.140
As it gets dark, your field of vision narrows.

443
27:18.141 --> 27:21.578
Running like that, you feel like you're going faster.

444
27:22.545 --> 27:24.013
It's an illusion of sorts.

445
27:24.547 --> 27:26.416
An illusion?

446
27:26.483 --> 27:29.519
Yeah. You don't actually get faster.

447
27:32.122 --> 27:33.089
And, Keito,

448
27:33.790 --> 27:37.927
stop the stopwatch just before Hayato finishes.

449
27:38.995 --> 27:42.098
Then tell Hayato his time improved.

450
27:42.866 --> 27:45.735
-But that's-- -A bluff, of course.

451
27:48.138 --> 27:52.275
What do you think Hayato needs right now?

452
27:56.479 --> 27:58.782
Confidence, maybe?

453
28:01.851 --> 28:04.988
You give him that confidence.

454
28:07.624 --> 28:11.161
So yesterday, I stopped the stopwatch a little early.

455
28:18.501 --> 28:22.205
But, Hayato, this time is real.

456
28:22.872 --> 28:24.874
Your time really did improve.

457
28:27.143 --> 28:30.246
Honestly, I had my doubts too.

458
28:31.881 --> 28:35.118
But it was just like Coach Kai said.

459
28:43.827 --> 28:46.129
Confidence, huh?

460
28:52.535 --> 28:53.870
Thanks, Keito.

461
28:54.971 --> 28:56.840
Sorry I made you worry.

462
28:58.741 --> 28:59.909
No!

463
29:00.743 --> 29:04.113
I'll go tell Coach Kai your time!

464
29:04.180 --> 29:06.382
Excuse me.

465
29:26.102 --> 29:27.136
Go!

466
29:29.239 --> 29:32.442
Last 5,000! Run at your own pace! Let's pick it up!

467
29:32.508 --> 29:33.743
Don't tense up!

468
29:38.114 --> 29:39.949
SEIWA INTERNATIONAL HEAD COACH KOICHI KITANO

469
29:40.016 --> 29:41.751
TOHO ECONOMICS HEAD COACH SEIJIRO ONUMA

470
31:21.050 --> 31:22.518
Good work!

471
31:23.720 --> 31:24.554
Good work.

472
31:24.620 --> 31:26.889
That last surge was amazing,

473
31:26.956 --> 31:27.924
Captain.

474
31:30.393 --> 31:31.361
Thanks.

475
31:33.363 --> 31:37.567
Those three always slack off when they train with us.

476
31:38.234 --> 31:40.370
How long are they going to keep that up?

477
31:49.846 --> 31:51.047
Got a minute?

478
31:55.151 --> 31:57.854
This team needs you three.

479
31:59.655 --> 32:01.157
So I want all three of you

480
32:01.758 --> 32:04.227
to give this team everything.

481
32:05.161 --> 32:07.430
I think you should take

482
32:08.064 --> 32:09.832
every workout seriously.

483
32:09.899 --> 32:11.601
I told you before.

484
32:12.902 --> 32:15.705
We'll do things our way.

485
32:16.205 --> 32:20.743
Just because you're captain doesn't mean you can push your opinions.

486
32:31.220 --> 32:32.622
Thanks, Captain.

487
32:36.592 --> 32:38.061
That run was nowhere near

488
32:38.694 --> 32:41.230
what you're capable of.

489
32:42.598 --> 32:43.800
Kota, why?

490
32:46.502 --> 32:47.537
Shuto.

491
32:51.707 --> 32:53.009
Seiya?

492
32:55.478 --> 32:57.713
You slack off for no reason?

493
33:01.717 --> 33:04.687
If you train with no clear purpose or motivation,

494
33:04.754 --> 33:06.155
don't bother.

495
33:06.756 --> 33:08.091
It's a waste of time.

496
33:10.193 --> 33:13.196
Figure out for yourselves what kind of athletes you should be.

497
33:13.262 --> 33:15.364
And what you have to do.

498
33:17.100 --> 33:18.267
Think about it.

499
33:27.143 --> 33:29.145
I don't think the way you handled

500
33:29.212 --> 33:31.347
Kota and the others was right.

501
33:32.381 --> 33:33.416
How so?

502
33:34.117 --> 33:37.653
They're much smarter than you think.

503
33:38.554 --> 33:42.391
You've been away a long time, so you may not understand.

504
33:42.458 --> 33:46.295
Students today aren't interested in anything unrealistic.

505
33:47.563 --> 33:48.931
Unrealistic, like what?

506
33:50.733 --> 33:52.135
I told you before.

507
33:52.835 --> 33:54.470
This team's goal.

508
33:56.973 --> 33:58.808
Why do you assume that?

509
34:00.343 --> 34:02.445
This is the Student United Team.

510
34:03.279 --> 34:04.580
Hakone isn't so easy

511
34:04.647 --> 34:07.083
that a thrown-together team can win it.

512
34:07.783 --> 34:10.786
Could you stop talking like an amateur?

513
34:10.853 --> 34:12.822
The runners know it too.

514
34:14.123 --> 34:16.526
I think the runners are on board.

515
34:19.162 --> 34:20.363
This is pointless.

516
34:25.201 --> 34:27.236
He won't get it right now.

517
34:27.870 --> 34:29.705
He's a serious man.

518
34:30.573 --> 34:33.910
But if you ask me, he's too serious and lacks ambition.

519
34:34.911 --> 34:37.046
He relies on experience and common sense

520
34:37.613 --> 34:39.382
and never questions his assumptions.

521
34:41.984 --> 34:45.087
But Mr. Kitano was right about one thing.

522
34:45.955 --> 34:48.491
That the runners are smart.

523
34:50.293 --> 34:52.828
I'm going to bet on that.

524
34:53.996 --> 34:55.932
TOHO UNIVERSITY OF ECONOMICS

525
35:06.909 --> 35:10.513
I'll now announce your legs.

526
35:12.648 --> 35:15.651
Based on the survey, your times,

527
35:16.285 --> 35:19.055
stamina and strengths,

528
35:19.121 --> 35:20.590
I've given each of you

529
35:21.157 --> 35:23.659
two possible legs for now.

530
35:23.726 --> 35:25.595
-Two? -Why?

531
35:25.661 --> 35:26.896
What do you mean?

532
35:30.199 --> 35:32.468
LIST OF LEGS

533
35:32.535 --> 35:34.503
The fourth leg and the tenth leg…

534
35:34.570 --> 35:36.505
Yes! I got the first leg!

535
35:36.572 --> 35:38.941
The fifth leg! Time to climb the mountain!

536
35:39.008 --> 35:39.942
You're loud.

537
35:40.009 --> 35:44.614
By the end of December, I'll decide the ten who'll run.

538
35:44.680 --> 35:46.649
As soon as you can,

539
35:47.216 --> 35:49.285
walk the course you'll run.

540
35:49.352 --> 35:50.353
JO INOMATA

541
35:50.419 --> 35:52.021
Study the course closely.

542
35:52.588 --> 35:54.890
The feel of the town. The road surface.

543
35:54.957 --> 35:58.728
Know the buildings at each kilometer. Drill it into your heads.

544
35:58.794 --> 36:01.664
Where the turns are, how the wind blows,

545
36:01.731 --> 36:02.632
the ups and downs.

546
36:02.698 --> 36:03.366
‎MAKOTO AMAGI ‎FIRST AND EIGHTH LEGS

547
36:03.432 --> 36:04.767
Note the sun's direction

548
36:04.834 --> 36:06.969
and how warm it gets.

549
36:07.036 --> 36:07.670
‎REN MINEGISHI ‎THIRD AND TENTH LEGS

550
36:07.737 --> 36:10.973
Then make your own race plan

551
36:11.040 --> 36:13.009
and figure out where it'll be decided.

552
36:13.075 --> 36:13.776
‎FUTA WADA-SECOND AND FIFTH LEGS

553
36:13.843 --> 36:16.112
Knowing the course inside out

554
36:16.679 --> 36:18.981
makes all the difference in the main race.

555
36:19.048 --> 36:20.383
KOTA MATSUKI FIRST AND NINTH LEGS

556
36:20.449 --> 36:21.851
Picture how you'd run it

557
36:22.418 --> 36:24.253
and where you'd make your move.

558
36:24.320 --> 36:26.622
Imagine and simulate it.

559
36:27.189 --> 36:28.357
One plan isn't enough.

560
36:29.125 --> 36:31.127
Picture several scenarios

561
36:32.094 --> 36:33.929
and think it through deeply.

562
36:34.830 --> 36:35.998
-Yes. -Yes.

563
36:41.370 --> 36:42.905
You two just won't give up.

564
36:43.572 --> 36:45.207
Why don't you ask Maruta?

565
36:46.642 --> 36:48.177
If we use Maruta,

566
36:48.944 --> 36:50.846
the Hakone Ekiden will change.

567
36:50.913 --> 36:53.115
He's adaptable enough.

568
36:53.182 --> 36:56.385
If he's asked, he'll fit in.

569
36:57.386 --> 36:59.455
Sappy directing is right up his alley.

570
37:00.256 --> 37:01.357
Mr. Karashima.

571
37:01.857 --> 37:06.462
We want to see the Hakone Ekiden with you calling it.

572
37:07.363 --> 37:08.998
We want viewers to see

573
37:09.065 --> 37:12.468
Dainichi TV's proudest Hakone Ekiden

574
37:13.069 --> 37:14.637
through Bunzo Karashima's eyes.

575
37:21.210 --> 37:24.613
"A sports broadcast announcer should be a traffic light."

576
37:25.915 --> 37:29.085
Mr. Maeda kept those words in mind

577
37:29.151 --> 37:31.554
as Hakone's broadcast announcer.

578
37:32.054 --> 37:34.724
Car One is with the leaders. How's Aoyama Gakuin looking?

579
37:34.790 --> 37:35.691
Mr. Okada?

580
37:35.758 --> 37:38.761
Igarashi of Aoyama Gakuin is slowing a little…

581
37:38.828 --> 37:40.329
Over to Car Two for a moment.

582
37:40.396 --> 37:43.599
From the studio, he directs traffic like a traffic light.

583
37:44.166 --> 37:47.303
He gets the announcers in the field to say

584
37:47.370 --> 37:48.871
the right things at the right time.

585
37:49.739 --> 37:51.407
"Don't make yourself the focus."

586
37:51.474 --> 37:55.811
"Let the right person deliver the most important information."

587
37:56.779 --> 37:59.448
"Mr. Karashima was the one who taught me that."

588
38:00.483 --> 38:03.219
That's what Mr. Maeda said.

589
38:08.090 --> 38:10.126
We absolutely need you

590
38:10.760 --> 38:13.963
to make the Hakone Ekiden a success!

591
38:18.367 --> 38:19.902
Can I say something?

592
38:22.538 --> 38:23.406
Maruta…

593
38:31.013 --> 38:32.081
Mr. Karashima.

594
38:37.186 --> 38:41.290
Will you be the broadcast announcer for the Hakone Ekiden?

595
38:45.394 --> 38:48.097
Mr. Kuroishi wants it to feel

596
38:48.164 --> 38:49.331
like a variety show.

597
38:50.800 --> 38:51.700
But…

598
38:52.668 --> 38:55.971
I know the Hakone Ekiden isn't that kind of broadcast.

599
38:57.940 --> 39:00.810
I did start out in sports, after all.

600
39:03.446 --> 39:05.548
Mr. Tokushige, you told me, right?

601
39:07.049 --> 39:09.084
TV must never change Hakone.

602
39:11.620 --> 39:13.389
I agree.

603
39:15.524 --> 39:18.160
No more and no less.

604
39:19.228 --> 39:23.299
I'm not the one who can show the Hakone Ekiden as it is.

605
39:24.800 --> 39:26.135
You are.

606
39:30.206 --> 39:34.076
I'm not ready to take Hakone's sash yet.

607
39:47.389 --> 39:50.860
Mr. Karashima, please.

608
39:53.162 --> 39:56.332
Honestly,

609
39:56.866 --> 39:59.902
getting drilled for two hours

610
39:59.969 --> 40:01.770
on my name in training was rough.

611
40:03.072 --> 40:03.906
But

612
40:04.740 --> 40:08.177
I still remember what you told me.

613
40:09.712 --> 40:11.914
"Your name is your calling card."

614
40:12.481 --> 40:14.650
"You have to say it better than anyone."

615
40:17.152 --> 40:20.890
I'm really grateful you were so strict with me.

616
40:22.191 --> 40:24.593
I want to learn from you again

617
40:25.628 --> 40:28.297
at Hakone.

618
40:30.266 --> 40:31.634
Mr. Karashima.

619
40:32.935 --> 40:34.670
I'm asking too.

620
40:34.737 --> 40:36.238
Please.

621
40:40.242 --> 40:41.243
Please.

622
40:56.058 --> 41:00.362
ANNOUNCING DEPARTMENT

623
41:12.207 --> 41:15.110
-Mr. Tokushige, do something! -What?

624
41:15.177 --> 41:17.947
That's right. We need him to say yes!

625
41:18.514 --> 41:20.516
Don't blame me!

626
41:21.350 --> 41:23.218
I'm trying too!

627
41:32.328 --> 41:34.863
-Tokushige. -Yes?

628
41:35.764 --> 41:36.899
Do you have it? That thing.

629
41:38.968 --> 41:40.736
That thing. Don't you know?

630
41:41.971 --> 41:43.572
The handbook.

631
41:44.139 --> 41:46.976
The Hakone Ekiden Broadcast Handbook.

632
41:51.080 --> 41:52.948
Get it to me by tomorrow.

633
41:54.550 --> 41:55.517
Okay.

634
41:58.153 --> 41:59.588
Well, I'm going to eat.

635
42:03.792 --> 42:05.094
Thank you!

636
42:06.895 --> 42:10.833
-Yes! -Thank goodness…

637
42:21.577 --> 42:24.046
That was close.

638
42:25.981 --> 42:28.584
I'm sorry about Hakone.

639
42:32.187 --> 42:37.593
I just think Mr. Karashima should be the broadcast announcer.

640
42:39.428 --> 42:40.262
I see.

641
42:43.599 --> 42:45.701
Well, it can't be helped.

642
42:46.402 --> 42:50.339
Let's both pray it's a good show.

643
42:52.541 --> 42:53.442
Yes, sir.

644
42:54.677 --> 42:55.611
Excuse me.

645
43:00.716 --> 43:02.618
MARUMARU HOUR BY SHINJI MARUTA

646
43:13.062 --> 43:13.996
Good work.

647
43:14.697 --> 43:15.831
Good work.

648
43:16.565 --> 43:18.901
What's this? It's upside down.

649
43:18.967 --> 43:20.602
You're right.

650
43:31.613 --> 43:35.551
HEAD COACH'S OFFICE

651
43:35.617 --> 43:36.552
Come in.

652
43:37.453 --> 43:38.554
Excuse me.

653
43:41.657 --> 43:42.791
Did you see this?

654
43:48.831 --> 43:49.832
MONTHLY TRACK &amp; FIELD MAGAZINE

655
43:49.898 --> 43:51.734
-Huh? -I knew it would

656
43:51.800 --> 43:53.502
come to this someday.

657
43:54.169 --> 43:56.972
I only agreed to coach

658
43:57.039 --> 43:59.608
because we placed 13th in the qualifying race.

659
44:00.275 --> 44:01.376
That's it.

660
44:03.145 --> 44:04.947
Why do I have to waste every weekend

661
44:05.013 --> 44:07.549
on this ridiculous training?

662
44:09.051 --> 44:09.918
Um…

663
44:11.253 --> 44:14.757
what should I do about practice starting next week?

664
44:14.823 --> 44:16.925
Decide that yourself.

665
44:18.227 --> 44:19.261
But

666
44:19.328 --> 44:23.332
if you don't go, I won't either.

667
44:27.703 --> 44:28.570
COACH SHOSUKE HIRAKAWA

668
44:28.637 --> 44:32.241
"Abolish the Student United Team"?

669
44:33.776 --> 44:36.945
What's the point of letting

670
44:37.012 --> 44:39.615
a Student United Team with no official record compete?

671
44:41.216 --> 44:43.619
Instead of adding a team like that,

672
44:43.685 --> 44:46.355
they should let another strong school run.

673
44:46.421 --> 44:48.924
At this point, it's just laughable.

674
44:55.497 --> 44:56.331
45 DAYS UNTIL HAKONE EKIDEN

675
44:56.398 --> 44:57.432
THIS DRAMA IS A WORK OF FICTION UNRELATED TO REAL PEOPLE OR GROUPS.
